//! Typed message-passing channels (`Channel<T>`).
//!
//! A `Channel<T>` is the thin Kyte-side handle over a channel object that lives
//! in the C++20 runtime. It is the primitive the actor model and the CSP-style
//! `spawn` code build on: one coroutine (or thread) hands a value to another
//! without sharing mutable state, so ownership passes cleanly across the channel.
//!
//! The design decision here is deliberate thinness. The struct itself holds only
//! an opaque `handle` (a `long`, because it is a raw runtime pointer or table
//! index and an `int` would truncate a 64-bit address) plus the `Allocator` the
//! caller wants values allocated from. All of the real work, the wait queue and
//! the bounded buffer, lives behind the four `kyte_channel_*` runtime intrinsics.
//!
//! IMPORTANT (threading model): `Channel<T>` is a THREAD primitive, not a
//! coroutine-suspending one. `send` on a full buffer and `recv` on an empty one
//! block the calling OS thread on a condition variable until the other end makes
//! progress. They do NOT yield to the async scheduler. Calling a blocking `recv`
//! or `send` on a reactor thread (for example from inside an `async fn` request
//! handler) freezes every coroutine on that reactor until a counterpart arrives;
//! the runtime detects this and aborts in a debug build (warns once in release).
//! Use `Channel<T>` to pass values between real threads (the `spawn`-onto-a-thread
//! and actor patterns). For producer/consumer hand-off WITHIN the reactor, use the
//! async-safe channel in `concurrency/asyncchan`, whose `recv` genuinely suspends
//! the coroutine and resumes it when a value arrives.
//!
//! Capacity chooses the rendezvous semantics. A capacity of `0` is an unbuffered
//! (synchronous) channel: a `send` does not complete until a matching `recv` takes
//! the value, so the two ends meet in a rendezvous. A positive capacity is a
//! bounded buffer: sends succeed without a waiting receiver until the buffer fills,
//! after which a further `send` waits for space.
//!
//! Lifetime is manual. The handle is a runtime resource, so the owner must call
//! [`Channel.delete`] exactly once when the channel is finished with; dropping the
//! handle without deleting leaks the runtime object, and deleting twice is guarded
//! against here by zeroing the handle. See the wider concurrency story in the
//! runtime's `concurrency.cpp` scheduler, and the actor and `spawn` helpers that
//! sit on top of this type.

import bytes;
import mem.allocator;
import assert;

/// A typed channel over which values of `T` are passed between concurrent tasks.
///
/// The struct is a lightweight handle: it owns a runtime-side channel object
/// referenced by [`Channel.handle`] and does not itself hold the buffered values.
/// Construct it with an initial capacity (0 for an unbuffered rendezvous channel,
/// or a positive bound for a buffered one) and an [`allocator.Allocator`], move
/// values through it with [`Channel.send`] and [`Channel.recv`], and release the
/// runtime object with [`Channel.delete`] when done.
pub struct Channel<T> {
    /// Opaque reference to the runtime-side channel object.
    ///
    /// This is a raw runtime pointer or table index, hence a `long` and not an
    /// `int`: a 64-bit address stored in an `int` would truncate to garbage. A
    /// value of `0` means the handle has been released (see [`Channel.delete`])
    /// and must not be used for any further send or receive.
    pub handle: long,
    /// The allocator associated with this channel for values it manages.
    ///
    /// Retained so the channel's memory needs can be satisfied from the same
    /// arena the caller chose, rather than an implicit global one.
    pub allocator: allocator.Allocator,

    /// Creates a channel with the given `capacity`, backed by a fresh runtime
    /// channel object.
    ///
    /// A `capacity` of `0` yields an unbuffered channel where each `send`
    /// rendezvouses with a `recv`; a positive `capacity` yields a buffered
    /// channel that accepts up to that many pending values before a `send`
    /// waits. The `alloc` allocator is stored on the struct for later use. The
    /// returned channel owns a runtime resource, so the caller must eventually
    /// call [`Channel.delete`].
    init(capacity: int, alloc: allocator.Allocator) {
        self.allocator = alloc;
        self.handle = kyte_channel_create(capacity);
    }

    /// Sends `val` into the channel, transferring it to a receiver.
    ///
    /// On an unbuffered channel this waits until a receiver is ready to take the
    /// value; on a buffered channel it completes immediately while space
    /// remains, and otherwise waits for room. The wait BLOCKS the calling OS
    /// thread on a condition variable, it does NOT suspend the coroutine (see the
    /// threading-model note in the module header). Never call this on a reactor
    /// thread; use `concurrency/asyncchan` for in-reactor hand-off.
    pub fn send(self: Channel<T>, val: T): void {
        kyte_channel_send(self.handle, val);
    }

    /// Receives the next value from the channel, waiting if none is ready yet.
    ///
    /// If the channel is empty this BLOCKS the calling OS thread on a condition
    /// variable until a sender provides a value; it does NOT suspend the coroutine
    /// (see the threading-model note in the module header). Never call this on a
    /// reactor thread; use `concurrency/asyncchan` for in-reactor hand-off. The
    /// value returned is the one handed over by the matching [`Channel.send`].
    pub fn recv(self: Channel<T>): T {
        return kyte_channel_recv(self.handle);
    }

    /// Releases the runtime-side channel object and marks this handle dead.
    ///
    /// This must be called exactly once by the owner when the channel is no
    /// longer needed, otherwise the runtime object leaks. It is safe against a
    /// double delete: the handle is checked for non-zero before destruction and
    /// then set back to `0`, so a second call is a no-op. After this returns the
    /// channel must not be used for [`Channel.send`] or [`Channel.recv`].
    pub fn delete(self: Channel<T>): void {

        if (self.handle != 0) {
            kyte_channel_destroy(self.handle);
            self.handle = 0;
        }
    }
}

/// Round-trips a single string through an unbuffered channel to check the basic
/// send/receive contract and clean teardown.
///
/// Uses a capacity of `0`, so the `send` and `recv` form a rendezvous within the
/// one task, then asserts the received value matches what was sent and calls
/// [`Channel.delete`] to release the handle.
@test
fn test_channel(): void {
    let alloc = allocator.globalAllocator();
    let ch = Channel<string>(0, alloc);

    ch.send("hello from channel");
    let val = ch.recv();
    console.log("DEBUG: val received = " + val);
    assert.isTrue(val == "hello from channel");
    ch.delete();
}
